>     The code is running painfully slow, on a fairly robust machine (Core i7, 32GB RAM, Linux, etc.)
>
>
> The hardware has less to do with the wall time than the software, and you've coded it in the slowest possible way for a
> model like this. The only real benefit for a CPU like that is that you can do 8 chains at once.
>
>     However, after 43 hours, it is about 50% complete.  Curious to see what some of the samples looked like, I had a
>     look at the samples.csv output file.  Much to my surprise, all of the samples appear to have the exact same values.
>     All of them - the same.
>
>
> The only thing that I can see that is really wrong with your code is that real eta[N] should be declared at the start of
> the model {} block, rather than inside that loop, i.e.
>
> |
> model {
>    real eta[N];
>    ...
> |
>
> but I don't think that would cause all the proposals to get rejected. I suspect that the default starting values are
> inappropriate for your data, but even then I wouldn't expect every proposal to be rejected.
>
> So, I would try taking out the group and program effects. If that model runs, add the group effects back. If that model
> runs, add the program effects back. Etc. That will help you narrow down the problem.
>
> Ben
