Tom:

We'd really like to help, but I'm too paranoid to run
binary files from users.  Please send us:

   1.  the model file and just the model file

   2.  the data file in R dump format

You can use the stan_rdump {rstan} command to create the
dump from data if you have it read into R.

If you just can't manage to get (2) working, I can use
a plain text R source script and data in something like .csv
format.  And if you can't get (2) working, I'd like to hear
why so we can fix stan_rdump or its doc.

In your case, I can cut the model out of the .R file, but
I'd rather not have to go fishing and cutting and pasting.
But the data appears to be read from a data.Rdata binary,
which I'm not willing to run.

In the meantime, have you tried running stan with flag

     test_grad=TRUE

in the stan() function call?  It will help you diagnose where there's
a problem with initial conditions if any by dumping out
all the unconstrained parameters and their gradients.  These should
all be finite and should not be too huge.  (We need to add better display
for the test_grad output -- right now, you need to realize the parameters are
listed in declaration order.)

Sorry for the inconvenience -- we really do want to help.

- Bob

